      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Vaginal cysts are a common finding. The differential diagnosis includes epidermal inclusion, Bartholin gland, Gartner duct, and Skene gland cysts and Müllerian anomalies. Rarely, leiomyomas, cancers, or ectopic ureters or megaureters are found."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CASE",
          "content": "A 31-year-old woman, gravida 3 para 3, presented with a 12-year history of intermittent abdominal pain and involuntary loss of urine. She was diagnosed with ectopic vaginal megaureter after evaluation and subsequently underwent a nephroureterectomy with a marsupialized distal ureter. She remains asymptomatic."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Consideration of a rare diagnosis such as an ectopic ureter is important when patients repeatedly present with involuntary urinary incontinence in conjunction with a vaginal cyst and an otherwise negative evaluation. Using magnetic resonance imaging can be useful to assess urinary tract involvement, particularly in patients with known urinary tract malformations."
        }
      ]},
